\chapter{What is weft?}
\pagenumbering{arabic}

\textbf{weft} is a small, language-agnostic tool for \emph{literate
programming}: you write one document that interleaves prose and code, and
weft turns it into two things --- a program a compiler can build, and a
document a human can read. It is a modern fork of Preston Briggs' nuweb,
rewritten for clarity and extended with Markdown output, source-mapped
section markers, and a toolkit for AI-assisted navigation.

If you have five minutes, read the next two sections and the
Quick Start (Chapter~\ref{ch:quickstart}); that is enough to write your
first literate program. The later chapters are a reference you can dip
into as needed.

\section{The idea in 30 seconds}

A literate program is a narrative. You explain \emph{what} you are
building and \emph{why}, and you drop the code into that explanation in
whatever order tells the best story --- not the order the compiler
demands. Each piece of code is a named \emph{fragment}; you assemble
fragments into files by reference.

weft reads that one source file (extension \verb|.weft|) and does two
independent jobs:

\begin{quote}
\begin{tabular}{@@{}lp{9.2cm}@@{}}
\textbf{tangle} & extract the code --- write out the real source files a
compiler or interpreter consumes. This is the default. \\[4pt]
\textbf{weave} & typeset the document --- produce a \LaTeX\ (\verb|.tex|),
Markdown (\verb|.md|), or HTML (\verb|.html|) file with the prose, the
code, and automatic cross-references. Turned on with \verb|-w|. \\
\end{tabular}
\end{quote}

\noindent The same source is both the program and its documentation, so
the two can never drift apart.

\section{Tangle and weave, side by side}

One \verb|.weft| file fans out into everything else:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
                      weft hello.weft  (tangle, the default)
                   +--------------------->  hello.c, util.c, ...
  hello.weft ------+                         (real source, compiled as usual)
  (prose + code)   |
                   +--------------------->  hello.tex --> PDF
                      weft -w tex ...        hello.md      (GitHub, ...)
                      (weave)
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

Tangling and weaving are independent: by default weft only tangles, so it
fits quietly into a normal build. Ask for a document with \verb|-w|, and a
single pass produces both.

\section{Why literate programming}

The premise, due to Knuth~\cite{Knuth:CJ-27-2-97}, is to stop writing
code with documentation bolted on and instead write documentation with
code woven in --- ``the difference between performing and exposing a
magic trick.'' Three things follow:

\begin{description}
\item[Order for humans, not compilers.] Present a program top-down,
bottom-up, or in ``stream of consciousness'' order --- whatever makes it
easiest to understand. tangle re-assembles it for the machine.
\item[Names instead of detail.] A fragment reference like
\hbox{\tt @@<Validate the request@@>} replaces a screenful of code with a
meaningful name, so structure is visible at every level.
\item[One source of truth.] Prose and code live in the same file and are
generated together; stale comments become impossible by construction.
\end{description}

\section{Why weft}

weft keeps nuweb's best trait --- it works with \emph{any} language, or
several at once --- and modernizes the rest:

\begin{description}
\item[Language-agnostic.] weft never parses your code, so C, Python,
Rust, SQL, shell, Protobuf, or a mix all work the same way. What it gives
up (automatic prettyprinting, a built-in identifier index) it trades for
simplicity and total control over layout.
\item[Three woven formats.] \LaTeX\ for a typeset book; Markdown
(\verb|-w md|) for documentation that renders on GitHub with no \TeX\
installation; and self-contained HTML (\verb|-w html|) for a browsable
page that opens in any browser.
\item[Source-mapped output.] Tangled files carry \emph{section markers}
and \verb|#line| directives, so a compiler error in the generated code
maps straight back to the \verb|.weft| source (Chapter~\ref{ch:debug}).
\item[A toolkit for tools.] A JSON project map (\verb|-m|), single-fragment
extraction (\verb|-e|), reverse source mapping (\verb|-R|), a linter
(\verb|--lint|), and compiler-error translation (\verb|--errors|) make
weft webs navigable by scripts and AI agents alike
(Chapter~\ref{ch:ai}).
\item[Small and fast.] The command set is tiny, and by default weft only
tangles, so it stays out of the way of your build.
\end{description}

\noindent The rest of this guide is practical: write a first program
(Chapter~\ref{ch:quickstart}), keep the Cheatsheet
(Chapter~\ref{ch:cheatsheet}) at hand, and reach for the reference
chapters when you need the details.
